# Derived empirically, not chosen: each option below was measured against the
# existing sources (10 representative files, 6107 lines) and kept only if it
# REDUCED the diff a reformat produces. Without any config clang-format falls
# back to LLVM style and rewrites 19.6% of lines; this gets it to 6.8%.
#
# Measured, added one at a time to this baseline — all made it worse, so none
# of them are here:
#   AlignConsecutiveAssignments: Consecutive   +512 lines  (aligns designated
#                                                initialisers the source leaves
#                                                unaligned — by far the worst)
#   AlignConsecutiveMacros: Consecutive        +178
#   AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: None      +76
#   AlignTrailingComments: Never/Leave       +72/+60
#   AlignEscapedNewlines: Left/DontAlign        +30
# And these changed nothing, so they are left at the LLVM default:
#   BreakBeforeBraces, PointerAlignment, IndentPPDirectives,
#   SpacesBeforeTrailingComments, MaxEmptyLinesToKeep,
#   AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine
#
# Re-derive with the sweep in the shell history if the codebase style shifts.
#
# TREE-WIDE PASS -- tools/dev/run_clang_format.sh (--check to verify only).
# The exclusions moved into that script so CI and a developer cannot drift
# apart on scope; its header carries the reasons (firmware/docs holds source
# snapshots that are evidence, .clang-format-ignore is silently ignored by the
# pip wheel). CI runs `--check` as a gate, pinned to clang-format-18.

BasedOnStyle: LLVM
ColumnLimit: 80

# The two that actually pay. Includes are grouped by meaning here, not
# alphabetically; and the comments are hand-broken prose that re-wrapping
# leaves starting mid-sentence.
SortIncludes: Never
ReflowComments: Never
